[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Objects: Properties and Methods"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In JavaScript, an object is a collection of key-value pairs, where keys are strings (or Symbols) and values can be any data type, including other objects, functions, and primitive values. Objects are fundamental building blocks in JavaScript, allowing you to represent complex data structures and behaviors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Properties"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A property is a characteristic of an object. It's essentially a variable that is attached to an object. You can access and modify properties using dot notation or bracket notation."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Creating an object
const person = {
  firstName: "John",
  lastName: "Doe",
  age: 30,
  isEmployed: true
};

// Accessing properties using dot notation
console.log(person.firstName); // Output: John
console.log(person.age);       // Output: 30

// Accessing properties using bracket notation
console.log(person["lastName"]); // Output: Doe

// Modifying properties
person.age = 31;
person["isEmployed"] = false;

console.log(person.age);       // Output: 31
console.log(person.isEmployed); // Output: false"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bracket notation is particularly useful when the property name is stored in a variable or when the property name is not a valid JavaScript identifier (e.g., it contains spaces or starts with a number)."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"const propertyName = "firstName";
console.log(person[propertyName]); // Output: John

const address = {
  "street address": "123 Main St"
};

console.log(address["street address"]); // Output: 123 Main St"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Methods"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A method is a function that is a property of an object. It defines the behavior of the object. Methods are invoked using dot notation or bracket notation, just like properties, but with parentheses () at the end to call the function."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"const person = {
  firstName: "John",
  lastName: "Doe",
  age: 30,
  greet: function() {
    console.log("Hello, my name is " + this.firstName + " " + this.lastName);
  },
  getAge: function() {
    return this.age;
  }
};

// Calling a method using dot notation
person.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is John Doe

// Calling a method using bracket notation
console.log(person['getAge']()); // Output: 30"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'this' keyword inside a method refers to the object that the method is being called on. It allows methods to access and manipulate the object's properties."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding and Deleting Properties and Methods"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can add new properties and methods to an object after it has been created. You can also delete existing properties and methods using the 'delete' operator."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"const person = {
  firstName: "John",
  lastName: "Doe"
};

// Adding a property
person.age = 30;

// Adding a method
person.sayHello = function() {
  console.log("Hello!");
};

person.sayHello(); // Output: Hello!

// Deleting a property
delete person.age;

console.log(person.age); // Output: undefined

// Deleting a method
delete person.sayHello;

//person.sayHello(); // This will cause an error because sayHello is no longer defined"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Object Literals vs. Object Constructors"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The examples above use object literals, which are a convenient way to create single objects. For creating multiple objects of the same type, you can use object constructors (classes in ES6 and later)."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Object constructor (ES5)
function Person(firstName, lastName, age) {
  this.firstName = firstName;
  this.lastName = lastName;
  this.age = age;
  this.greet = function() {
    console.log("Hello, my name is " + this.firstName + " " + this.lastName);
  };
}

// Creating objects using the constructor
const person1 = new Person("John", "Doe", 30);
const person2 = new Person("Jane", "Smith", 25);

person1.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is John Doe
person2.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is Jane Smith"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// ES6 Class
class Person {
  constructor(firstName, lastName, age) {
    this.firstName = firstName;
    this.lastName = lastName;
    this.age = age;
  }

  greet() {
    console.log("Hello, my name is " + this.firstName + " " + this.lastName);
  }
}

const person1 = new Person("John", "Doe", 30);
const person2 = new Person("Jane", "Smith", 25);

person1.greet();
person2.greet();"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding objects, properties, and methods is crucial for writing effective JavaScript code. They allow you to organize and manipulate data in a structured and reusable way."}]
